WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.170
QUIQUE TOLOZA:
I'm Quique Toloza,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.870
and at the McGovern Institute,
I use computer models

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.670
to study the brain.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.590
When I was in middle
school, I put together

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.050
my first computer out
of spare components

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.030
we had lying around the house.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.830
I was really into
video games and wanted

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.480
to play on my own PC.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.450
Video games are actually why
I first got into programming.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.420
I wanted to change the rules
of those virtual worlds

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.010
to fit my imagination.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.470
I would spend hours
trying to reverse engineer

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.620
how little simple snippets of
code interacted with each other

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.460
to make the characters on the
screen behave the way they did.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.740
It's really not that
different from what I do now.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.780
I build computer models
to try to understand

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.970
how and why the brain
works the way it does.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.280
And there's a beautiful
and often challenging

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.170
thing about working
with code in this way.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.570
Just like in the
best video games,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.110
the main limit to what I can
create is my own imagination.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

